                      tnx - thanks for the error report

  Syntax:     tnx=code,number,action,#num
              tnx=list

  Response:   tnx/code,number,action,#num,string,example


Settable parameters:
  code     Two character error code, e.g., 'sp', or 'list' to show the status
           of all "recognized" errors (see Comments about 'tnx=list').

  number   Error message number, e.g., '-999'.

  action   Display status: 'on', 'off' (default), 'force' ('forc'), or
           'unforce' ('unfo'). Only the first four characters are checked.
           'force' sets all errors with 'code,number' to 'off'. 'unforce'
           removes an error set with 'force'. (See Comments about 'force' and
           'unforce'.) In responses, only 'off' and 'on' can occur.

  #num     literal '#' followed by an integer associated with the error
           "string". Default is '#0', which will select the only instance of
           an error 'code,number'. Negative values match all instances of
           'code,number'. In responses, '#0' indicates an error set by
           'force'.

Monitor-only parameters:
  string   the error message instance, in curly braces, '{}',
           associated with 'code,number' and '#num'. This is null if the
           'code,number' is not defined in 'fserr.ctl' or 'sterr.ctl'. It can
           only be non-null if 'example' is null. Any curly braces within
           'string' are not escaped, but should be rare.

  example  example of message if not defined in 'fserr.ctl' or 'sterr.ctl'.
           It can only be non-null if 'string' is null. Any curly braces
           within 'example' are not escaped, but should be rare.

Comments:
This command is intended to provide a way to turn off display of specific
error messages if the operator determines that nothing can be done to correct
the problem. For example, if one parameter of a module's set-up is being
reported incorrectly by the hardware, but has been checked and is known to be
correct, the operator can suppress that one error message rather than
unchecking the entire module. Even if the error message is suppressed for
display, it is still written to the log.

WARNING: This command must be used with great care. If misused it may turn off
important error messages that the operator needs to know about. Consequently,
its use (except for just 'tnx' and 'tnx=list') is blocked if it is executed
from a procedure, schedule, or time-list. This command must be entered
interactively (in a pinch, the 'op_stream' command or the 'inject_snap'
program can be used to workaround this restriction). The list of suppressed
error messages is cleared every time the FS is started. TURNING OFF AN ERROR
MESSAGE IS NOT THE SAME AS CORRECTING THE UNDERLYING PROBLEM, WHICH IS THE
PREFERRED METHOD OF DEALING WITH AN ISSUE.

Normally, an error must have occurred before its display status can be
manipulated with 'tnx'. However, for 'force' any previous instances of
'error,code' are removed and all future instances are set to 'off', regardless
of whether it has occurred yet or not. This provides two features: (i) it
allows turning off an error in advance for an unattended systems if its being
reported will cause a problem (perhaps needlessly waking someone in the
middle-of-the-night) and (ii) turning off all instances of 'code,number'. The
display status of an error set with 'force' can be manipulated with 'on' and
'off'.

The action 'unforce' can be used to remove a 'code,number' error set with
'force' so that its display status can be manipulated normally after the next
time the 'code,number' error occurs; in other words, you are starting over
from scratch for that 'code,number'. Consequently, using 'force' followed by
'unforce' removes the history of error instances for that 'error,code'.

If the 'action' parameter is 'on' display of the message is re-enabled.

Use of the "previous value" symbol "*" is not supported for any parameters of
"tnx".

The '#num' parameter is only needed if more than instance of an error with the
specified 'code,number' has occurred. If there is more than one instance and
'#num' is not specified, all instances that have occurred so far are displayed
with their corresponding 'num' values along with a suggestion to use the
'#num' form. If '#num' is negative, it will match all instances of the
'code,number' that have occurred so far. In this case only, the FS will not
complain if the display status of any of code,number errors are already in the
requested state.

Using just 'tnx', will display all errors that have been suppressed. Each
error is listed in a separate log entry. Errors displayed with '#0' have been
set with 'force'. 'string' and 'example' will both be null until the first
instance of each has occurred.

The form 'tnx=list' will show the status of all "recognized" errors. The
recognized errors are all the encountered errors plus errors set with 'force'
and not removed with 'unforce'.

If the displayed error 'string' is null (empty) in the listing, but when the
error occurs a string is displayed that is because the programmer is supplying
the string in a call to 'logite()' along with the error 'code,number'. This
may be because the string contents are variable. Note that in this case 'tnx'
will not distinguish between such variable strings. It will suppress all
errors with that code,number'. This is different from how it handles
different error strings from 'fserr.ctl' and 'sterr.ctl' (when 'force' was not
used), all of which are individually suppressible, but have a more limited
range of variation. For 'code,number' errors  with completely variable error
strings, the 'tnx' command prints an 'example' string to help identify it.
This is most helpful if all the errors for this 'code,number' are related,
differing only by a specific details in an otherwise fixed string.
